RESPONSE
My essay will be focussed on the religious tradition of the Catholic Church.

In the 21st century, there has often been dispute within religious traditions about whether certain beliefs and practices need to be changed in order for the tradition to maintain its authenticity and relevance with modern day expectations. This has led to the reconsideration and changes of certain systems, and the possible discussion around what the beliefs in a religious tradition actually mean. Diversity, equity, and inclusion are a critical framework of values implemented into systems to achieve fairer and more just societies. So when the Catholic Church, a religion known for its social justice and inclusion is scrutinized as to whether it actually upholds the values of DEI in its own tradition, this leads to large discussions and proposals within the church on how it can change to honor the values of diversity, inclusion and equity.

Part 1 (Example of a change that has BEEN introduced): Inculturation
A key example of how the Catholic Church has discussed and changed practices in its religious traditions has been the development of inculturation in the Church over time.
The scriptural basis of inculturation comes from two very commonly cited biblical sources: Genesis 1:27, which discusses how humans are "made in the image and likeness of God" and forms Catholic teaching on why every individual has inherent dignity and should be treated with love and respect. As well as, Romans 12:3-4 in which St Paul describes the church as "The body of Christ" where because each member is a different part each has a "different function" pointing out the importance of difference under the universal 'Christian' identity. And it is these two guiding sources that helped point the way of discussion towards inculturation in the Catholic Church in the 20th Century.
Because Catholicism at the time was becoming more globalised and less 'European-centred', the major point of discussion became: "Are the practices we have still relevant to the church we have today?" Something that was heavily considered in Vatican II, held by Pope John XXIII, which created a lot of discussion around whether allowing for inculturation would break up the 'universal' Catholic identity, or further strengthen it. Ultimately, the result of this discussion lead to reformation in the use of vernacular in liturgy as well as the inclusion of cultural hymns and praise.
Another example of inculturation was in the early missionary work and encouragements of Bishop Pompallier by using Te Reo when teaching local Maori communities about the Catholic faith. Through Maori hymns such as Mo Maria, or translating the bible into Te Reo, not only was the missionary work more effective, but it also helped adapt the practices of local Catholic communities to be more relevant to the country and its people.
A Catholic Theologian, Robert Schreiter argues that inculturation is necessary in local church for the community to be fair and inclusive. Proposing three aspects that can be used to properly implement inculturation and measure the success of the outcomes: 1) The inculturation must be for those of the community AKA authenticity cannot just be 'faked' 2) The inculturation must help strengthen the faith of those who are a part of the community. 3) The inculturation must also be relevant to the Catholic's mission & social justice. And it is these three aspects that we see work together in the two previous historical examples of inculturation. They show that the dispute around whether allowing for difference would maintain a universal Catholic identity was solved and is an example of how practical changes were made to uphold the calls for better DEI in the Catholic practices.

Part 2: Women in the Catholic Church
A large point of dispute within the Catholic Church is the equality of women and their authority in the church. The scriptural basis for gender equity being found in Galatians 3:28 "...Neither male nor female" where St Paul speaks of everyone being equal under Christ. And in the Catechism of the Catholic Church 2334, where the church sets out that both "men and women are equal" and both deserve equal respect. However the structural rule that women cannot be ordained as priests and thus have no sacramental authority, is something that many people argue does not allow for diversity, inclusion and equity.
In Ordinatio Sacerdotalis published in 1994, Pope John Paul II confirmed that the Catholic Church "does not have the authority to" and cannot ordain women. Backing this up with "Apostolic Succession" where priests, because Jesus conferred the authority to his disciples must be men for them to act in "persona christi" for them to celebrate the liturgical sacraments. However this clashes with the Episcopalian beliefs, which argue that gender is not necessary for priesthood, because the function of clergy is for "service" and that 'persona cristi' does not necessary mean that liturgical celebrants have to be male. These differences in evaluation of beliefs have lead to different institutional structures of both religious traditions, but has also created a variety of proposals and discussions around the equality of women in the church. There have been a wide range of proposals and discussion, some of those of which being:
Elizabeth Shussler (a German theologian) coined the term "Kyriarchy" where she described the structure of the Catholic Church as being an exclusionary patriarchy. She discusses that while the beliefs and core theological teachings of the church promote women as equal to man, it is the institutional systems that are what prevent women from having a truly equal place in the church. She argues that because women do not have access to liturgical authority, it is not possible for there to be true equity due to the institutional power that priests have.
Leonard Boff offers a different take. He recognises that women are equal, but also takes it further, basing his argument on the ways Jesus interacted with women. He notes that the distinction that Jesus made with his male disciples and female followers did not necessarily mean that men had greater authority than women, but that the authority that each counterpart held was different. He proposed therefore, that women don't necessarily recieve Apostolic Succession, but instead deserve a different form of institutional authority to repair the current inequity he claims the Catholic Church currently has.
Lastly, the current Catholic beliefs were discussed by Pope Francis, who argued that women do not need sacramental authority to have equality. He argued this through his implementations of putting women in roles that do not require institutional ordination, trying to bridge the gap between fully male-based power structures. He also created more inclusion, by allowing women to speak and voice their opinions in Synods, meaning that there was futher diversity and inclusion in the discussion of church matters. Each of these arguments and examples show that there is discussion within Catholicism about the role and equality of women in the church. Each view the issue from different perspectives and suggest/create proposals for change, making sure that modern expectations to honor (diversity, equity and inclusion) for a fair and relevant church community are being upheld but also aligned with scripture.

Part 3: LGBTQI+ inclusion in the Church
Another large point of dispute is the proper inclusion of LGBTQ+ individuals in the church, with the tension arising from whether the church is actually inclusive if it condemns the expression of non-heterosexual acts.
The basis of these arguments come from the Catechism of the Catholic church and the scriptural fundamental of Imago Dei (Genesis 1:27). In CCC: 2358, the Catholic Church argues that because every person is made in the image and likeness of God, it means that "homosexual individuals" should be treated with love, respect and sensitivity. Meaning that anyone, no matter their sexual orientation deserves equal treatment and inclusion. However, in CCC: 2357 it discusses acts of homosexuality as "intrinsically disorderd" and "acts of grave depravity". And it is this basis that has lead to the saying "Love the sinner but hate the sin" meaning that the Catholic Church views each individual as deserving of love and respect, but does not accept any acts that do not follow those of heterosexual sex. An idea that some argue is not truly inclusive, and claim that it is a form of "conditional belonging" because the church does not accept the entirety of the matter itself.
In 2023, the Vatican published Fidus Supplicans, where it allowed priests to bless same-sex couples, however it did not mean that the union of the couples themselves could be blessed. This declaration is an example of a change that took a step towards promoting for the inclusion of individuals that are LGBTQ+ whilst maintaining the theological grounding that the church has. However, within the Catholic Church it created a lot of mixed opinions. For the Catholic Bishops of Africa, they rejected Fidus Supplicans, unlike the Catholic Synod Way in Germany which argued that this change was not enough, and that further steps had to be taken, both of these communities likely influenced by the political and cultural beliefs of the area. And it is this declaration that demonstrated how much dispute Catholic followers had in how to properly accomodate and include individuals and couples of LGBTQ+.
Sister Elizabeth Johnson (a feminist theologian) proposed that for LGBTQ+ individuals to be properly included into the church, a reinterpretation of scripture was necessary. By using the 'Dulle's toolkit' she argued that previous interpretation was influenced by historical bias and stigma, claiming that there was evidence for full acceptance of homosexuality in the bible scriptures. Claiming that because God created everything and said that it was good, so too must any aspect and expression of an individual that is LGBTQ+ be (good).
However a completely different argument was proposed by philosopher Germain Grisez. According to his theory of Natural Law, morality is defined for what is good for humans (eg: life, play, work etc). And argued that because the act of heterosexual sex is one that is open to procreation and possibility of new life, and because homosexuality cannot do so, it is therefore an immoral act. He argues that it is not neccesary for LGBTQ+ individuals to express their sexual desires for them to be included, because the immorality of the action justifies the need to reject it.
Both these individuals argue for and against the morality of the homosexual act, in the aim of forming an understanding so as to better find accomodation to suit individuals of an incredibly diverse LGBTQIA community. They highlight the different perspectives that the Catholic Church can consider, and highlight the need for further discussion and changes. Not just for the sake of maintaining relevance, but also to grow the understanding of Catholic followers on an issue that many may not be aware of.

